  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 332 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nWeak upper-level ridging over the region will allow warmer and drier \nconditions to continue on Sunday. High temperatures are expected to \nrange from the upper 80s to mid 90s for most locations. A low chance \n(10-20%) of isolated storms is prevalent across the Davis Mountains, \nthanks to upslope winds. A more active weather pattern is in store \nbeginning on Monday as another upper-level system approaches the \narea. Long-range guidance has what is going to be the remnants of \nHurricane Polo, will eventually be pulled into the aforementioned \nsystem supplying more moisture to the region. As a result, areawide \nrain chances are expected by Monday night lasting through Wednesday. \nEnsemble guidance also suggests PWATs reaching near and even \nexceeding daily maximum climatological values Tuesday evening. \nTherefore, the threat of heavy rainfall leading to flash flooding \nreturns. Uncertainty still remains in timing, positioning, and \nimpacts of the potential heavy rainfall. We will continue to monitor \nthis upcoming system closely. A Pacific front is forecast to move \nthrough the region sometime on Wednesday bringing breezy westerly \nwinds and cooler temperatures spanning from the upper 70s to 80s. \nDrier and slightly warm temperatures (mid to upper 80s) are expected \nbehind the front Thursday and Friday. \n\nLamberson",
